```python
import heapq

def k_largest(nums, k):
    return heapq.nlargest(k, nums)
```

`heapq.nlargest` keeps a heap of size k: it makes one pass over the data, maintaining only the k best candidates seen so far. That's O(n log k) time and O(k) extra memory. Sorting everything is O(n log n) — when k is small relative to n (top 10 of ten million), the heap wins decisively, and the gap grows with n. When k is close to n, sorting is simpler and just as fast, so use `sorted(nums, reverse=True)[:k]`. Rule of thumb: if you need *all* the data ordered, sort; if you need a small leaderboard from a big stream, heap — `nlargest` also works on any iterable, including generators you can only read once.

```python
def max_window_sum(nums, k):
    if k > len(nums):
        return None
    window = sum(nums[:k])
    best = window
    for i in range(k, len(nums)):
        window += nums[i] - nums[i - k]
        if window > best:
            best = window
    return best
```

The naive approach sums each window from scratch: O(n·k). The sliding window observes that consecutive windows differ by exactly two elements — one leaves on the left (`nums[i - k]`), one enters on the right (`nums[i]`) — so each step is O(1) and the whole scan is O(n). Compute the first window in full, then slide. This pattern generalizes: fixed-size windows over sums, averages, and counts are all one running total plus an add and a subtract per step.

